WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.269
Maps are very hard to make.

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:32.290
To create a map from scratch, one has to do
years of field work or analyzation of satellite

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:36.710
photos so plagiarism has always been rampant
among mapmakers.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:42.050
It’s pretty easy to just redraw the same
geographical features from one map onto a

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.530
new map, and it hard to get caught.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.590
You are, after all, drawing the exact same
world.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:53.080
So mapmakers came up with a way to catch individuals
stealing their data.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.960
This is Argleton in West Lancashire, England.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:00.710
According to this 2009 screenshot of Google
Maps, Argleton is a town.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:05.900
It’s supposedly located between Aughton,
to the south and Ormskirk, to the north.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.930
The problem is, if you go to this site, this,
is what you find.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:14.020
Aughton was placed on the map as a paper town.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.810
Paper towns are are fake places that are put
on maps as copyright traps.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:23.780
A mapmaker can make up a place, and if they
see that a competing mapmaker also has that

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:29.020
town on their maps, they know that at one
point the competitor plagiarized their info.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.020
So in Argleton’s case, the town probably
wasn’t placed there by Google.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:37.880
Google gets their data from Tele Atlas who
sources their data from other mapping companies,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.160
who in turn get some of their data from other
mapping companies.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.770
Somewhere down the line, someone must have
placed Argleton on the map as a trap and its

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.580
presence on Google Maps was just a remnant
of that.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:55.340
After the fake town gained widespread media
attention, Google removed it from their maps

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.970
and it has since been replaced with a marker
designating the spot as a “ghost town.”

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.890
There are hundreds of other examples of paper
towns.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:09.200
In New York, a spot north of Roscoe, New York
was marked as the town of Agloe by the General

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.580
Drafting Company in 1930.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.420
The name came from an anagram of the cartographers
initials and appeared on numerous maps since

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:17.840
its conception.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.980
In the 1960’s, Agloe showed up on a Rand
McNally map of the region and, thinking that

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.880
they had fallen into their trap, Esso, who
had bought the map from the General Drafting

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.110
Company, threatened to sue McNally.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:33.990
The suit was dropped, though, when Rand McNally
pointed out that Agloe had actually become

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.820
a real place.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:40.830
Sometime in the 1950’s, locals opened up
a General Store north of Roscoe, New York,

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:45.720
and, after looking at an Esso map of the region,
decided to call their store the Agloe General

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.590
Store, therefore making Agloe an official
place.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:53.710
The store eventually went out of business
and the town has since disappeared from most

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:57.720
maps, but, this was one instance where a fake
town became real.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.260
A similar idea to paper towns has been used
in other domains.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:07.600
In 2001, the word esquivalence appeared in
the New Oxford American Dictionary defined

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.870
as "the willful avoidance of one's official
responsibilities.”

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.960
That word was made up as a copyright trap,
and… it actually worked!

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.580
dictionary.com and google’s dictionary both
added a listing for the word esquivalence,

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:26.770
possibly proving a level of plagiarism among
the online dictionaries.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:32.010
Occasionally, unintentional traps are created
that prove effective.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:37.790
Before they created Apple Maps, Apple was
making maps on a much smaller scale for iPhoto.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:41.980
These maps had no attributions and therefore
no one can be sure where their data was from,

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.080
but there were some obvious clues.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:50.520
This screenshot of Apple’s map of Norwich
shows an area of disorganized roads to the

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.520
northeast.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.950
If you look at the Open Street Map map of
the area, you can see that these roads are

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.290
actually trails in a park.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:01.690
Overlaying the Apple map over the OSM map
of the area, you can see how perfectly the

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:04.520
supposed roads and trails line up.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.790
You can assume from this that Apple took their
map data from Open Street Map and mistook

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:10.850
the trails for roads.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:15.990
A few months after the release, Apple officially
credited Open Street Map for their contributions

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:21.160
to the maps confirming that they used OSM’s
data.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.230
So those are paper towns.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.009
I hope you enjoyed this weeks episode.

00:04:24.009 --> 00:04:27.659
I’ll be back next week to bring you more
from that wikipedia list.

